(function () {
  if (typeof BRANDS_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var EN = {
    "Casa Turrent": {
      band: {
        check: "When the core line was renamed Serie 1901, the wording \"Serie 1901\" was added on a second band."
      },
      founded: "2013 (unveiled at Mexico's cigar festival, with the flagship line marketed as Serie 1901; by family tradition, tobacco cultivation began in 1880)",
      founder: "The Turrent family. Alberto Turrent (b. 1942) and his son Alejandro Turrent (b. 1973) lead A. Turrent / Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos. The family counts five generations; tradition holds that great-great-grandfather Alberto Turrent planted tobacco at San Andrés in 1880 (another genealogy has an Antonio arriving from Barcelona in 1790)",
      meaning: "\"House of Turrent\". The numbers of the principal lines (1901, 1942, 1973) mark the birth years of the current head's grandfather, father and the head himself, while \"1880\" commemorates the year the family began growing tobacco",
      strength: "Medium–Full in body. The Serie 1901 gives sweet spice, cedar, nuts, leather and white pepper. The 1880 spans a range of strengths from Claro to Oscuro, the Oscuro being the most powerful of them",
      status: "An active premium brand, made at the Turrent family's Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos / Casa Turrent factory, with company lounges in Mexico City's Polanco and Santa Fe districts",
      history: "【The Turrent Family's Flagship Brand】\nCasa Turrent is the premium line produced by the Turrent family, the first family of Mexican cigars. In deliberate contrast to Te-Amo, long known for its inexpensive, mass-market image, it was conceived to show the world the heights of quality that tobacco from the Valle de San Andrés can attain. The operation is led by Alberto Turrent and his son Alejandro Turrent through A. Turrent / Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos S.A. de C.V., a family enterprise now in its fifth generation. The company owns more than 1,000 acres of tobacco fields near San Andrés Tuxtla in the state of Veracruz, ranks among the region's largest employers, and is Mexico's largest cigar manufacturer.\n【1880 — or an Even Older Origin】\nBy family tradition the story begins in 1880, the year in which Alejandro's great-great-grandfather Alberto Turrent is said to have crossed from Cuba to Mexico carrying a small quantity of tobacco seed and to have planted the first tobacco fields in the fertile Valle de San Andrés in south-eastern Veracruz. A more detailed genealogy reaches further back, holding that Antonio Turrent arrived from Barcelona in 1790, that his descendant Antonio Turrent Cinta was the first to devote himself to commercial tobacco growing, and that his son Alberto Turrent Vázquez married a woman of the Carrión family in the late 1890s. The sources agree that 1880 is the year the family took up tobacco cultivation, though the dates of arrival and the counting of the generations vary. A dormant volcano rises nearby, and the rich soil and ideal climate have long nurtured fine tobacco.\n【From Leaf Merchant to House Brand】\nFor four generations the Turrents owned no cigar brand of their own, building their strength instead as leaf merchants supplying other manufacturers. Their tobaccos — above all San Andrés Maduro wrapper of the Morron strain — are said to have served as key components of some of America's most popular premium brands, among them Montecristo, Padron and Macanudo. The San Andrés Morron Maduro leaf the family specially developed is today widely acknowledged as one of the world's finest wrappers, and as a pioneer of San Andrés wrappers and unusual blends the company has long stood apart from other producers.\n【The Birth of Casa Turrent】\nCasa Turrent began in 2013, when a maduro cigar was unveiled at the Festival del Puro Mexicano, Mexico's cigar festival. The cigar subsequently became the house smoke of the A. Turrent lounge in Mexico City (in the upmarket Polanco district), and after limited releases in Canada and parts of Europe the flagship line was christened Serie 1901. Serie 1901 reached international markets in 2013 and the United States in August 2015. Where Te-Amo was a cigar within reach of the many, Casa Turrent was conceived as a flagship of an entirely different price and character, distilling the finest of the family's cultivation skills.\n【Lines Numbered for the Generations】\nThe numbers of the principal lines each mark a milestone in Turrent family history. Serie 1901 derives from the birth year of the current head's grandfather, Serie 1942 from that of his father Alberto Turrent, and Serie 1973 from that of the current head Alejandro Turrent, while \"1880\" is dedicated to the year the family began growing tobacco. The portfolio was overhauled in December 2015 with the addition of Serie 1942 and Serie 1973 (some sources date the roll-out to 2016); with this, the existing flagship line was renamed Serie 1901 and a second band reading \"Serie 1901\" was added. This consistent nomenclature, in which the numbers themselves form a family tree, sharpens the narrative character of Casa Turrent.\n【Blend and Vitolas】\nSerie 1901 combines a San Andrés Maduro wrapper with a double binder of San Andrés Criollo and Nicaraguan leaf, and a filler mixing San Andrés Criollo and Negro with Nicaraguan tobacco. High-priming leaves grown on the family's own farms are aged for more than four years before use; the smoke is medium to full bodied, with sweet spice, cedar, roasted nuts, leather and white pepper trailing into a long, creamy finish. The range includes a newly added Torpedo (5 1/2 × 50), while Serie 1942 and 1973 offer a Robusto (5 1/4 × 50), Gran Robusto (6 × 52) and Doble Robusto (5 1/4 × 56). The Serie 1901 Robusto scored 94 points in the American magazine Cigar Aficionado and placed twelfth in its ranking for 2016, praised as a fine example of the richness of San Andrés leaf.\n【Casa Turrent 1880】\nThe top-of-the-range Casa Turrent 1880 line was introduced at the end of 2017. It comprises four wrappers/blends — Claro, Colorado, Oscuro and Maduro — all built around San Andrés tobacco. The Claro pairs a Habano Criollo San Andrés claro wrapper with a Negro San Andrés binder and Mexican and Dominican fillers. The Colorado, somewhat darker and stronger, carries a Negro San Andrés colorado wrapper over a Habano Criollo San Andrés binder and Mexican filler. The Oscuro, darker still, is composed almost entirely of Habano Criollo San Andrés apart from its Negro San Andrés binder. The wrappers undergo five years' ageing and the binders and fillers ten to twelve — an extravagant construction. Initially offered only as a Toro (6 1/2 × 54–55), the line gained a Lancero (7 1/2 × 40) at the 2021 PCA. Priced at about US$20 a cigar in boxes of ten, it is not a limited edition but is made in deliberately restricted quantities, with a single pair of rollers assigned to each blend and monthly output capped at 5,000 cigars. Cigar Aficionado awarded the 1880 Maduro 91 points and halfwheel 90, the latter judging that it \"balances sweetness and strength and almost never becomes unpleasant\".\n【Origins and the World Stage】\nThe Origins series was first shown at the InterTabac trade fair in Dortmund in 2018, made its US debut the following year and reached Germany in early 2020. It consists of four cigars bearing the names of places central to the Turrent company's story — Cuba, San Andrés, Nicaragua and Miami — each offered in a single Robusto Extra format (5 1/2 × 54) at about US$7 in boxes of twelve. The San Andrés is an all-San Andrés cigar using Negro San Andrés for wrapper, binder and filler, medium to full in flavour with earth, cacao and woody notes. By sealing the memory of an origin into each cigar, the series extended Casa Turrent's storytelling still further.\n【Lounges and the Present】\nCasa Turrent also maintains its own lounges as points of sale. To the Polanco premises in Mexico City it added, in June 2014, a large shop in the bustling Santa Fe district, which proved popular. Where Te-Amo built a popular reputation, Casa Turrent has opened a new axis of appraisal for Mexican cigars as a premium brand crystallising five generations of accumulated expertise as leaf merchants. Emblematic of this was Cigar Journal's feature entitled \"Mexico's Finest Cigars\", for which the magazine interviewed Alejandro Turrent and toured the Mexico City shops and lounges. That a single family fields both Te-Amo and Casa Turrent — two flagships utterly different in price and character — speaks of the Turrents' commanding presence in the Mexican cigar industry.\n【The Global Standing of the San Andrés Wrapper】\nCasa Turrent's true worth lies in its deep understanding of San Andrés wrapper leaf. The maduro leaf of this region, also called \"Morron\" or \"Negro\", takes on a naturally dark, powerful hue and is known for dense flavours suggesting chocolate, coffee and earth. In recent years cigar makers the world over have clamoured for San Andrés wrappers — yet it is the Turrents who have grown that leaf for more than a century and supplied it to others. Casa Turrent is, in effect, the family's own answer to the question of what happens when the supplier dresses its cigars in the finest of its own wrappers.\n【A Philosophy of Ageing】\nWhat defines Casa Turrent is workmanship that begrudges no time. In the 1880 line the wrappers are aged for five years and the binders and fillers for ten to twelve, and even Serie 1901 uses high-priming leaves rested for more than four years. No haste, no chasing of volume, a single pair of rollers per blend and strictly capped monthly production — a stance at the opposite pole from Te-Amo, which conquered its market through mass output. That the same family embodies at once the two values of popularity and rarity shows the breadth of the Turrent house.\n【Elevating the Mexican Cigar】\nMexican cigars once languished under a cheap, \"blue-collar\" image, and even during the cigar boom of the 1990s found themselves in the ironic position of being shunned for their very inexpensiveness. Casa Turrent is also an attempt to rewrite that reputation itself. The high marks from Cigar Aficionado, and the fact that Cigar Journal saluted \"Mexico's finest cigars\", demonstrated that Mexican tobacco can be discussed on the same footing as Cuban, Nicaraguan or Dominican. Casa Turrent became the vanguard reclaiming Mexico's pride as a cigar origin.\n【Summary】\nOnce leaf merchants supporting other houses from the shadows, the Turrents captured the masses with Te-Amo and the connoisseurs with Casa Turrent, taking both poles of the Mexican cigar into their own hands. Engraving the family's growth rings since 1880 into its line numbers, and resting its leaf unhurriedly for five and ten years at a time, Casa Turrent has become the most eloquent modern statement of the latent power of San Andrés tobacco. From the acclaimed 91–94-point scores of Serie 1901 to the top-flight 1880 line, the portfolio reads like a map charting, step by step, the heights Mexican cigars can reach. From the land of the cheap \"taxi-driver's cigar\" to an origin of premium cigars admired by connoisseurs worldwide — that transformation has been led from the front by five generations of the Turrent family and by Casa Turrent.",
      vitolas: [
        "Serie 1901 Robusto (rated 94 by Cigar Aficionado; No. 12 cigar of 2016)",
        "Serie 1901 Gran Robusto (6 × 52)",
        "Serie 1901 Torpedo (5 1/2 × 50)",
        "Serie 1942 Robusto (5 1/4 × 50) / Gran Robusto (6 × 52) / Doble Robusto (5 1/4 × 56)",
        "Serie 1973 Robusto / Gran Robusto / Doble Robusto (named for the birth year of current head Alejandro)",
        "1880 Claro (Habano Criollo San Andrés claro wrapper; Mexican and Dominican fillers)",
        "1880 Colorado (Negro San Andrés colorado wrapper)",
        "1880 Oscuro (almost entirely San Andrés Habano Criollo)",
        "1880 Maduro (rated 91 by Cigar Aficionado, 90 by halfwheel)",
        "1880 Lancero (7 1/2 × 40; added at the 2021 PCA)",
        "Origins San Andrés Robusto Extra (5 1/2 × 54; entirely Negro San Andrés)",
        "Origins Cuba / Nicaragua / Miami (Robusto Extra)"
      ],
      trivia: "Casa Turrent 1880, though not a limited edition, is made in restricted quantities: a single pair of rollers is assigned to each blend and monthly output is capped at 5,000 cigars per blend. The wrappers undergo five years' ageing and the binders and fillers ten to twelve, and the cigars sell for about US$20 each in boxes of ten. The Turrents' San Andrés Morron Maduro leaf is said to have been used for the wrappers of celebrated brands such as Montecristo, Padron and Macanudo, and five generations of accumulated experience as leaf merchants crystallise in this premium line."
    },
    "Ornelas": {
      founded: "Unknown. Companies of the name — Tabacos Ornelas S.A., Empacadoras de Puros Ornelas and Distribuidora de Tabacos Ornelas — appear in business directories in Guadalajara, in the Mexican state of Jalisco; empty Ornelas cigar boxes from the 1970s circulate on the antiques market; and the marque figures in the founding story of Te-Amo (in the Cuban-embargo era), suggesting it existed by the 1960s at the latest. Retailers' claims of \"more than 60 years in Mexico\" are consistent with this, but no primary source establishes an exact founding year",
      founder: "Unknown. The brand name appears to derive from the surname of a founding family, Ornelas. Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara is verifiable as a company, but no source naming an individual founder has been found",
      meaning: "Ornelas is a family name of the Spanish-speaking world. The line name \"English Market Selection\" (EMS) is a traditional cigar-trade term for a naturally brown wrapper finish, neither claro nor maduro, signalling an unflavoured, natural-finish series of serious intent",
      strength: "Mild–Medium (according to retailers' material). The unflavoured natural line (the English Market Selection) is reckoned a smooth smoke carrying nuances of liquorice, cream and cedar. A separate sweetened and flavoured stream (vanilla, cognac) also existed",
      status: "A venerable minor brand about which firm information is scarce. In North America it has circulated through online retailers such as Cuban Crafters and Factory Direct Cigars, but new releases are almost never reviewed in the specialist press. Within Mexico, Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara remains on the business registers even as enthusiasts ask whether the cigars can still be had, and notices have recently appeared for a launch event styled a revival of Puros Ornelas (\"EL REGRESO DE UNA LEYENDA\"). Overall a quiet presence, with present-day information strikingly thin beside its former renown",
      history: "【Overview, and the Nature of This Entry】\nOrnelas is a hand-made premium cigar brand of Mexico. According to North American retail literature, it blends Mexican tobacco as its base with aged Nicaraguan and Dominican Cuban-seed long fillers, rolled in a natural sun-grown Cuban-seed wrapper. The smoke is described as mild to medium, gentle in character, with flavours of liquorice, cream and cedar.\nA caveat is required at the outset, however. No official company history and no reported feature in the specialist press can be found for this brand, and the descriptions of its taste and blend derive almost entirely from the product copy of North American retailers. On the other hand, material corroborating the brand's existence and age does exist outside the retail literature. What follows deliberately keeps these two kinds of information distinct.\n\n【The Verifiable Side — Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara】\nWhat can be confirmed first is the fact that cigar companies bearing the name Ornelas have really existed in Guadalajara, in the Mexican state of Jalisco. Business directories list Tabacos Ornelas S.A. at 214 Calle Alfredo R. Placencia in the city's Ladrón de Guevara district, with Empacadoras de Puros Ornelas registered at the same address as a retailer of cigars, cigarettes and tobacco; a company database further records Distribuidora de Tabacos Ornelas S.A. The goods listed in the directories — \"fine cigars, cognac-finished LTD, Orneles 1 to 6, Matiné large and small\" — suggest a core range of numbered sizes, a flavoured offshoot line and a small matiné format.\nA second strand of evidence is the presence on the antiques market of empty Tabacos Ornelas boxes from the 1970s, showing that the brand has existed for at least half a century. Old threads entitled \"Ornelas - LTD\" and \"Puros Ornelas\" on Spanish-language cigar forums show that it retains a certain place in the memory of Mexican smokers. The retailers' phrase \"more than 60 years in Mexico\" is consistent with all this — though, conversely, the phrase itself is promotional copy of unknown date and cannot on its own establish a founding year.\n\n【Ornelas in the Shadow of Te-Amo's Birth】\nThe most vivid appearance of the Ornelas name in Mexican cigar history comes in the founding story of Te-Amo. According to the Mexican cigar press, in the era of the Cuban embargo an American businessman, Robert Luzgarde, visited Guadalajara, smoked an Ornelas cigar there and was deeply impressed. He investigated the origin of its leaf and bought a small cigar workshop of six artisans in San Andrés Tuxtla. Speaking little Spanish, he took on a partner from Nuevo León and built a proper factory, but died a few years later; his heirs sold the facility together with the newborn \"Te-Amo\" brand. Seeing an opportunity, Alberto Turrent Carrión, together with the young Alberto Turrent Cano, acquired brand and factory in the late 1960s — the starting point of what would become Mexico's flagship marque.\nIf the tale is accurate, Ornelas is no mere obscure minor brand: a single Ornelas smoked in Guadalajara set in motion the marque that came to symbolise Mexican cigar exports to the United States. The account rests, however, on a single Mexican publication and lacks independent corroboration; it is best treated as anecdote.\n\n【Position within the Mexican Cigar Industry】\nThe Mexican cigar industry grew up around the San Andrés Tuxtla valley in the state of Veracruz, home to two great families. One is the Turrents, tobacco growers since the 1880s and owners of Te-Amo and Casa Turrent (their factory being Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos). The other is the Ortiz family, said to have been involved with leaf tobacco since the 1830s, whose Santa Clara 1830 — founded by Jorge Ortiz in 1968 — is styled the oldest surviving cigar factory in San Andrés.\nWhereas both of these powers are rooted in Veracruz, the corporate presence of Ornelas is in Guadalajara, in the western state of Jalisco. Guadalajara is an old tobacco entrepôt where cigar and cigarette factories operated even under the Spanish royal tobacco monopoly of the eighteenth century, and it belongs to a lineage separate from San Andrés. Whether the Ornelas products exported to North America were made in Guadalajara or by a contractor in Veracruz cannot be determined from the sources, nor is there proof that the two are the same undertaking; Ornelas being a common surname in Mexico, identity of company name alone is no basis for a firm conclusion.\n\n【Blend and Method — a Hybrid, Not a Puro】\nAccording to retail literature, the Ornelas design rests on prime Mexican tobacco, with aged Nicaraguan and Dominican Cuban-seed leaf blended in as long filler, rolled in a natural sun-grown Cuban-seed wrapper. All the cigars are handmade (hecho a mano), and the raw material is said to be well aged before use.\nWhat should be noted here is that this construction is not a Mexican puro. Mexican cigars traditionally prided themselves on being all-Mexican \"puros\", but the current Ornelas description explicitly claims a multi-origin blend — the idea being to round a Mexican backbone with foreign leaf for balance, evidently aiming at approachability in export markets. The result is described as a smooth, mellow smoke without excessive strength or rough edges, carrying nuances of liquorice, cream and cedar; it is placed as mild to medium, weighted towards drinkability and a gently sweet finish rather than heft. Whether this blend description applies to current production, or to what period, cannot be judged from the product copy.\n\n【The Natural Line — English Market Selection】\nThe core of the Ornelas range in North America is the unflavoured flagship line, the \"English Market Selection\" (EMS). EMS is a term long used in the cigar trade, originally denoting brownish (natural) wrappers selected for the British market — leaf of natural finish, neither claro or lighter in shade, nor maduro or darker. In the traditional scheme the light greenish candela was called \"AMS\" (American Market Selection) and the darker, more matured blackish brown \"maduro\", with EMS placed between them.\nEach cigar is housed in an individual cedar tube, and twenty-five are hand-packed into a cedar box. Representative sizes include the No. 1 (7 × 43, cedar-wrapped) and the Robusto (4 1/2 × 50, cedar-wrapped). In contrast to the flavoured offshoots, the designation signals a serious series foregrounding the natural flavour of the leaf itself. That so small a brand should employ such classical terminology suggests an aspiration to align itself with the Cuban tradition.\n\n【The Flavoured Line — Vanilla, and a Blow from Cigar Aficionado】\nOrnelas is also remembered for its sweet, flavoured side. In September 1996 Cigar Aficionado tasted the Ornelas No. 1 Vanilla and awarded it a lowly 69 points. According to the magazine's later retrospective \"20 Years of Tastings\", that 69 ranks among the lowest scores in its tasting history, cited alongside the Canary Islands' Penamil No. 57 (also 69) as one of its rare failures. On the 100-point scale anything under 70 is classed as \"below average\", so 69 was in effect a failing mark. The article recalls that both brands disappeared from the market soon after these sub-par verdicts, though they had sold well before. Given the influence the magazine's tasting pages held over the market at the height of the 1990s cigar boom, the verdict was likely no small wound to the brand.\nWhile the sweet flavouring found no favour with serious critics, it was embraced by beginners and the sweet-toothed — the vanilla line of Ornelas epitomises the divided fortunes of flavoured (aromatic) cigars. Nor was vanilla the only flavoured strain: the \"LTD al cognac\" (cognac-finished LTD) listed in the Guadalajara directory represents the same idea taken in another direction. The existence of coffee-flavoured products (\"Ornelas Cafeteros\" and the like) could not be confirmed in the present research.\n\n【Ageing and Packaging】\nOne hallmark of Ornelas — again per the retail literature — is the weight it places on ageing both raw material and finished cigars. Some stock is advertised as having continued to mature in its boxes for more than eight years since manufacture. Long ageing of cigars is held to soften the sharp edges of young leaf and round out the aroma, cedar serving through the process to hold humidity and fragrance. Housing each cigar in its own cedar tube and hand-packing them into cedar boxes appears intended to encourage such long maturation; the tubes also protect the cigars in transit and lend a presentation fit for gifting. In addition, vintage tinned products and 1970s wooden boxes survive on the market as objects of collection — for a brand so poorly documented, such packaging is among the few physical records conveying its former state.\n\n【Distribution and Present State — a Quiet Void, and Stirrings of Revival】\nOrnelas has been distributed through North American online retailers such as Cuban Crafters (cccrafter.com) and Factory Direct Cigars. New releases are hardly ever reviewed by the major critical media, and the brand has kept its distance from the industry's main stage. The flavoured vanilla line appears to have withdrawn after the low ratings of the 1990s, leaving chiefly the natural EMS as remaining stock in North America.\nThe situation within Mexico is harder still to see. While Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara remains on the business registers, a celebrated Mexican cartoonist could post on social media, \"Let me ask my Guadalajara friends once more: what became of Ornelas cigars? Do they still exist? Can they be had anywhere?\" — so obscure have ordinary channels of supply become. At the same time, a notice has been confirmed for a presentation by a Mexican cigar brand entitled \"PRESENTACIÓN DE PUROS ORNELAS. EL REGRESO DE UNA LEYENDA\" (\"the return of a legend\"), suggesting that some form of revival may have been under way; its sponsor, date and products remain unconfirmed, however, and judgement is reserved.\n\n【Summary】\nOrnelas, though a minor Mexican brand whose official history remains obscure, cannot be ignored on several counts. First, as a cigar company that really existed in Guadalajara it has left traces spanning more than half a century. Second, it has left its name, as the trigger, in the founding story of Te-Amo, a national marque. Third, the tale of its vanilla line — enshrined in Cigar Aficionado's tasting history among the lowest marks ever given — has, ironically, sustained its renown. And fourth, it adopted a hybrid design marrying Mexican leaf with foreign fillers, a departure from the puro tradition.\nThis entry has given priority to distinguishing explicitly between what has been established and what has not. It has not padded its length with conjecture, and wherever a statement rests solely on retailers' promotional copy, that has been noted in place.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\n- The founding year is unknown. \"More than 60 years in Mexico\" is North American retail copy of unknown date, without primary-source support.\n- The founder's name is unknown. No source could be found to support the \"Antonio Ornelas\" of an earlier draft, and he is therefore omitted here.\n- There is no proof that Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara and the \"Ornelas\" brand sold in North America are the same undertaking; the link is strongly suggested but not an established fact.\n- The manufacturing plant and operator are unknown; whether production lay in Jalisco or Veracruz cannot be determined.\n- The blend (Mexican plus Nicaraguan and Dominican fillers; Cuban-seed sun-grown wrapper) rests solely on retail product descriptions, without official confirmation from a maker and of unknown date; the judgement that it is \"not a puro\" likewise depends on that retail material.\n- The claim of \"more than eight years' ageing\" is likewise from retail literature.\n- Coffee-flavoured products (Ornelas Cafeteros etc.) could not be confirmed; the flavoured strains verified are the vanilla assessed by Cigar Aficionado and the \"LTD al cognac\" of the Guadalajara directory.\n- The size names \"Orneles 1 to 6\" and \"Matiné large and small\" come from business directories; dimensions and specifications are unknown, as is their correspondence to the North American No. 1 and Robusto.\n- Ornelas's role in the Te-Amo founding story rests on a single Mexican cigar publication; nothing, including the spelling of the name Robert Luzgarde, has been independently verified.\n- The launch notice proclaiming \"the return of a legend\" is confirmed to exist, but its organiser, date, products and the brand's underlying rights are not, and whether the brand is currently in production is unknown.",
      vitolas: [
        "No. 1 (English Market Selection, 7 × 43) — each housed in an individual cedar tube, 25 hand-packed to a cedar box; per North American retail listings",
        "Robusto (English Market Selection, 4 1/2 × 50) — likewise cedar-wrapped, in cedar boxes; per North American retail listings",
        "English Market Selection (collective name of the unflavoured natural flagship series) — said to blend Mexican leaf with Nicaraguan and Dominican Cuban-seed long fillers under a natural sun-grown Cuban-seed wrapper; a hybrid blend, not a puro (source: retail literature)",
        "No. 1 Vanilla (Ornelas No.1 Vanilla) — flavoured line; scored 69 by Cigar Aficionado in September 1996, on record as one of the lowest marks in the magazine's tasting history; now thought scarcely obtainable",
        "LTD (cognac-finished) — listed among the goods of Tabacos Ornelas of Guadalajara as \"ltd al cognac\"; a flavoured offshoot of unknown dimensions and specification",
        "Orneles 1 al 6 / Matiné grande y chico — domestic Mexican size ranges likewise drawn from the business directories; apparently a numbered core series plus small matinés, dimensions and blends unknown",
        "Vintage packaging (1970s wooden boxes, tinned products) — surviving on the antiques and auction markets as collectors' items; exact sizes uncertain"
      ],
      trivia: "What engraved the Ornelas name in cigar history was, ironically, one of the lowest marks on record. In September 1996 Cigar Aficionado awarded the flavoured No. 1 Vanilla just 69 points; in the magazine's \"20 Years of Tastings\" that score is cited, alongside the Canary Islands' Penamil No. 57, as one of the rare failures in its tasting history. The article recalls, though, that both brands had sold well before vanishing soon after the low verdicts. Yet Ornelas may have had another, far greater role. By an anecdote told in the Mexican cigar press, an American businessman visiting Guadalajara in the embargo era was so struck by a single Ornelas that he traced the origin of its leaf and bought a small workshop in San Andrés Tuxtla — out of which came Te-Amo, the national marque that later passed to the Turrent family. The story rests on a single source, however, and remains unverified."
    },
    "La Prueba": {
      founded: "1864 (the founding year of the operating firm Balsa Hermanos; some sources say \"between 1864 and 1870\". One record has the La Prueba brand established in 1898 with a branch opened in Puebla, so the dates conflict)",
      founder: "The Balsa brothers of Asturias — Ramón Balsa y del Río and José Balsa y del Río — who learnt Havana cigar making in Cuba before crossing to Veracruz to found the firm",
      meaning: "In Spanish \"La Prueba\" means \"the proof\" — also \"the trial\" or \"the test\". The building that housed the factory is said to have been bought when it was still planned as the Casino Veracruzano",
      strength: "No firm judgement can be made, modern assessments of the smoking character being wanting. The house made Havana-style puros from seed brought over from Cuba and leaf from its own estates, and is reckoned to belong to the classical Cuban-style lineage",
      status: "A historic brand now all but extinct. It operated in its historic premises until 1987 and closed in 1993 after an unresolved strike; thereafter the union and some ten former employees carried the machinery to another building and kept up small-scale hand production, but reports of October 2019 described the brand as on the verge of disappearing. The historic building survives, near-derelict, in Veracruz's historic centre, attracting cultural interest as a candidate for preservation and restoration",
      history: "【Overview】\nLa Prueba is a historic cigar (puro) brand founded in the port of Veracruz, Mexico, its operating firm being Balsa Hermanos (the Balsa Brothers). Established in the latter half of the nineteenth century by the Asturian Balsa brothers, who had learnt cigar making in Cuba, it exported to Europe and the United States at a scale said to have reached 50,000 cigars a day, and was for a time styled \"the most prosperous tobacco and cigar factory in the Americas\". Production ended in 1993 and the brand is now effectively all but extinct, but its building and history are handed down as an important part of Veracruz's cultural heritage. In Spanish, \"La Prueba\" means \"proof, trial, test\".\n\n【The Balsa Brothers and Their Cuban Apprenticeship】\nThe founders were the brothers Ramón Balsa y del Río and José Balsa y del Río. Natives of Asturias in northern Spain, they went young to Cuba, worked on the island's tobacco plantations, and then entered the manufacture of Havana cigars, already of worldwide renown. Nineteenth-century Asturias sent many of its young men across to the New World in search of fortune, and the Balsa brothers belonged to that migratory current. When the independence movement rose on the Caribbean island, the brothers resolved to move to Veracruz, relying on an uncle who had emigrated before them and become a wealthy merchant. For men leaving Cuba's turmoil for the Gulf coast of Mexico, their Cuban-trained cigar craft was their greatest capital, and it became the foundation of La Prueba's later quality. In transplanting the methods of Havana to Mexican soil, La Prueba may be called a direct descendant of Cuban cigar culture.\n\n【Founding in the Port of Veracruz】\nOn arriving in Veracruz the brothers are said to have begun making cigars of the very highest quality in a small shop in a corner of the mercado de Trigueros. In time they acquired a building and named it, as a formal cigar factory, \"La Prueba\". Sources differ on the founding date: some put the establishment of the firm (Balsa Hermanos) at 1864, others between 1864 and 1870, while another record holds that the La Prueba brand was established in 1898 with a branch in the city of Puebla. The building that housed the factory stood on the block running from the corner of Zamora and Hidalgo streets through to Lerdo street, and is said to have been bought by the brothers while still intended as the Casino Veracruzano. The factory faced the Parque Ciriaco Vázquez.\n\n【The Golden Age — 50,000 Cigars a Day and an Export Network】\nThe business grew rapidly. Around 1901, it is recorded, more than 350 artisans worked in two great halls known as galeras (rolling galleries), producing 50,000 cigars a day. By the early twentieth century the firm had become a major enterprise of 500 employees, with its own woodworking (box-making) shop. In an era of hand rolling, 50,000 cigars a day was an astonishing figure, and on the strength of this scale the enterprise was called \"the most prosperous tobacco and cigar factory in the Americas\". Veracruz was Mexico's oldest and largest port city, linked directly to Europe by the Atlantic sea lanes — an ideal situation for an export industry. Besides the home market, production was exported to Britain and Central America and on to the United States and Europe. Its fame reached the dignitaries of many nations: a cigar chest made specially for the purpose is said to have accompanied a presentation to Queen Isabella of Spain (an anecdote to be treated with reserve). With the port town's prosperity, the cigars of La Prueba became the pride of Veracruz and part of its identity.\n\n【Estates and Raw Material】\nLa Prueba used seed brought from Cuba and owned its own tobacco fields. With the success of the business, the Balsa brothers are said to have acquired an hacienda at Valle Nacional in the state of Oaxaca, land well suited to tobacco growing. Valle Nacional was known from the late nineteenth into the early twentieth century as one of Mexico's foremost tobacco districts and a source of fine leaf. A vertically integrated system — from leaf procurement through manufacture to box making, all within the firm — sustained quality and volume together. Cuban seed raised on their own land, rolled by Cuban-trained artisans: the whole chain was nothing less than an attempt to reproduce Havana's standards in Mexico, and it underwrote La Prueba's renown.\n\n【Several Brands, and Flor de Balsa】\nThe firm deployed several brands and marks, the records naming \"Balsa Hermanos\", \"Balsa Hnos.\", \"Balsa Hermanos Sucrs. S.A.\", \"La Prueba\" and \"Glorias de Colón\". Above all, its export flagship was \"Flor de Balsa\", made for the United States, Europe and the wider world. Primary sources detailing the individual vitolas (sizes) are scarce, but these marks together constituted the firm's range.\n\n【Decline — Strikes and the Closure of 1993】\nIn the later twentieth century La Prueba entered a long decline. A want of vision in carrying its products to new markets, and the presence of a union locked in labour disputes, are cited as causes of the factory's fall. Production in the historic building continued until 1987, but the firm then slid into a strike that found no resolution, and in 1993 the factory closed. The house founded in 1864 thus brought down the curtain on a production history of more than a century.\n\n【Rescue by the Union, and After】\nAfter the closure, the union (and some ten former employees) salvaged part of the machinery from the original factory and quietly continued hand production in another building (said to be on Reforma avenue, among other accounts). By October 2019, however, press reports described the brand as on the verge of extinction. The house once acclaimed first of the continent had shrunk to a state in which its lifeline was barely kept by a handful of artisans.\n\n【The Building's Legacy and Cultural Endeavours】\nThe historic factory building survives, near-derelict, in Veracruz's historic centre, the object of repeated appeals for preservation and restoration. Around the long-abandoned building, local lore of the supernatural (tales of hauntings and superstition) has even grown up, and it has drawn public attention as an emblem of the port city's memory. Scholarly and cultural interest in La Prueba runs high: the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM) has produced research on restoring the building for modern uses, and in 2019 the Veracruz Institute of Culture (IVEC) published Marta García de Benaglio's \"Historia de la Fábrica de Puros La Prueba\" (\"History of the La Prueba Cigar Factory\"). The book gathers images of the building, period advertising, lithographs and employees — an effort to record an industrial heritage slipping away. The newspaper El Universal reported its rise and fall in 2019 under the title \"de la gloria a la nostalgia\" (\"from glory to nostalgia\"). The sight of the once-peerless factory now an empty shell is repeatedly taken up in reporting and photography as a symbol of the rise and fall of Mexico's modern industry.\n\n【Assessment and Standing】\nLa Prueba is best placed not as a marque circulating in today's international cigar market but as a historic brand symbolising the prosperity of nineteenth-century Veracruz. In producing Havana-style cigars in quantity and at high quality on the basis of Cuban-trained skill and its own estates, and exporting them to Europe and America, it was a pioneering presence in Mexican cigar history. In 1883 the records of Mexico's Ministry of Development (Secretaría de Fomento) note honours conferred on Mexican tobacco industrialists including Ramón Balsa. Material for judging the smoke by modern standards is scant and the details of its taste cannot be stated with confidence, but the name is assuredly preserved in building and literature.",
      vitolas: [
        "La Prueba (the firm's flagship brand; Havana-style puros. Primary sources detailing the individual vitolas are scarce)",
        "Flor de Balsa (the export flagship for the United States, Europe and the wider world; part of the 50,000-a-day output was devoted to this marque)",
        "Glorias de Colón (one of the marks the firm deployed)",
        "Balsa Hermanos (Balsa Hermanos / Balsa Hnos. / Balsa Hermanos Sucrs. S.A. — marks bearing the firm's name, forming part of the range)",
        "The presentation chest for Queen Isabella of Spain (a bespoke cigar chest said to have accompanied a gift to the queen; an anecdote to be treated with reserve)"
      ],
      trivia: "At its height La Prueba employed more than 350 artisans in two galeras (rolling halls) and is recorded as producing 50,000 cigars a day. It farmed its own estates from seed brought out of Cuba and even kept a box-making workshop — a vertically integrated enterprise. In 1883 the records of Mexico's Ministry of Development note honours for founder Ramón Balsa, and exports reached Britain, Central America, the United States and Europe. Tradition also tells of a bespoke cigar chest presented to Queen Isabella of Spain. In 2019 the Veracruz Institute of Culture published a book recording the firm's rise and fall."
    },
    "Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos (Tabacalera Alberto Turrent)": {
      founded: "The Turrent family began growing tobacco in 1880 (under Alberto Turrent I, who settled from Spain). No primary source stating the year of incorporation of \"Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos S.A. de C.V.\" could be found; accounts date entry to the US market to 1964, and the start of Te-Amo production to 1963 (company website) or 1966 (English-language sources). Company literature claims \"more than 120 years in the Mexican tobacco industry\"",
      founder: "Alberto Turrent I (the founder, who settled in the San Andrés valley of Veracruz from Spain in 1880). The business is now run by the fifth-generation Alberto Turrent and his son, the sixth-generation Alejandro Turrent",
      meaning: "\"Nueva Matacapan\" is Spanish for \"New Matacapan\". Matacapan is a real Classic-period site and settlement in the valley of the Río Grande de Catemaco, between San Andrés Tuxtla and Catemaco, believed to have been Teotihuacan's trading outpost on the Gulf. The public trading name Tabacalera Alberto Turrent carries the founder's name",
      strength: "As to the character of the leaf the company grows, San Andrés Negro (the dark leaf) is thick and rich in oils and withstands heavy fermentation, which suits it to maduro wrappers and binders. Criollo, Sumatra-seed and Connecticut-seed leaf are also cultivated, and the finished cigars range from the medium typified by Te-Amo to the Medium–Full of the Casa Turrent lines",
      status: "Active. Headquarters and factory in the Sihuapan district of San Andrés Tuxtla, Veracruz (Avenida Tabamex). Mexico's largest grower of cigar tobacco and at once the country's largest cigar manufacturer and exporter, making Te-Amo (brand owned by Altadis), Casa Turrent, A. Turrent, Matacán, Andrea's and Legado San Andrés, among others",
      history: "【A Company Born of Spanish Settlement】Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos, S.A. de C.V., based at San Andrés Tuxtla in the Mexican state of Veracruz, is the country's largest grower of cigar tobacco and its largest cigar manufacturer. It is also known publicly by the name of its founder as Tabacalera Alberto Turrent, both names denoting the same Turrent family enterprise. The story begins in 1880, when Alberto Turrent I, arriving from Spain, settled in the Valle de San Andrés at the foot of the dormant volcanic massif of Los Tuxtlas and began growing tobacco. Potassium-rich soils laid down by the volcanoes over long ages, and humid winds rising off the Gulf of Mexico, nurture thick, oily, dark leaf. For six generations since, the Turrents have never left the valley, building the standing that Cigar Aficionado calls \"the first family of the Mexican cigar industry\". From the late nineteenth century into the twentieth, many tobacco dynasties in Mexico rose and vanished, but this family is reckoned effectively the only old house to have carried its business through the political and economic upheavals.【The Name — the Ruins of Matacapan】The \"Matacapan\" of the company name is an ancient settlement in the valley of the Río Grande de Catemaco, between San Andrés Tuxtla and Catemaco. Archaeology knows it as a trading outpost that Teotihuacan established on the Gulf from about AD 300 until around 800, believed to have been a node passing marine produce and the tropical goods of the Maya lands to the central highlands. \"Nueva Matacapan\" — \"New Matacapan\" — thus lays a modern tobacco enterprise over the memory of a valley that has been a centre of trade and produce for nearly two millennia. That the factory stands at Sihuapan, its address on an avenue called \"Avenida Tabamex\" preserving the name of the former state tobacco corporation, likewise illustrates the layered history of the place.【The Reality Behind \"Mexico's Largest Grower of Cigar Tobacco\"】The most important point in understanding the company is that, before it is a cigar brand, it is above all a great agricultural concern. Father and son, Alberto and Alejandro, are the largest growers of cigar tobacco in Mexico, and Cigar Aficionado writes that \"the San Andrés maduro leaf they grow outside Veracruz is world-renowned, and their varied tobaccos have become important components of many of the most popular premium brands in the United States\". A Cigar Journal field report on the growing region details plantings of 40 hectares of Habano Criollo, 100 hectares of San Andrés Negro and 30–40 hectares of Sumatra-seed. What deserves care here is the structure by which the supplier of the \"San Andrés wrapper\" used on cigars the world over and the maker of house brands coexist within one and the same company: it is not unusual for the wrapper of a Nicaraguan- or Dominican-made maduro in an enthusiast's hand to be leaf harvested in this valley.【1963: the Birth of Te-Amo and the US Market】On the house-brand side, the greatest achievement is Te-Amo. By the company website's account production began in 1963, with exports to the United States starting the following year; many English-language sources give a \"1966 debut\", so the starting point carries some latitude. Either way, in the 1960s, with the void opened in the American market by the embargo on Cuban cigars, all-Mexican-leaf puros flowed in as an apt substitute. Around the north-eastern cities — New York, Chicago, Pennsylvania — Te-Amo became a fixture of retailers' humidors, and there was reportedly a period when the United States bought about 75 per cent of the company's production. The Te-Amo trademark is now held by Altadis U.S.A., but manufacture has remained throughout with Nueva Matacapan, and in the relaunch Altadis staged at the 2024 TPE (Te-Amo The Original San Andres Valley Cigar) the manufacturing factory was expressly named as Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos.【A Many-Layered Brand Structure】The company's official site lists as its brands Te-Amo, Matacán, A. Turrent and Andrea's, and additionally carries the name Legado San Andrés. To these is added Casa Turrent, the premium line launched through its own distribution from 2015. With Casa Turrent holding the top international tier, Te-Amo the traditional core and Matacán the budget bundles, a single factory covers the whole span of price — a rational design given the scale of Mexico's cigar industry, and a breadth made possible precisely because the company grows its own leaf.【Factory and Community】By Mexican press accounts, the factory generates about 200 direct jobs and more than a thousand indirect ones in the harvest season. Over 60 per cent of employees are women, and the economic reach is said to extend to a dozen or more communities around San Andrés Tuxtla. That cigar making here has taken root less as industry than as a rural livelihood is a Mexican trait somewhat distinct from the great factories of Cuba or Nicaragua. Te-Amo is said to be distributed in 29 countries, and the factory has continued to serve as the narrow channel through which this inland farming district reaches the world.【The Tariff Wall, the Age of the Puro, and 1996】What defined the twentieth-century Mexican cigar was the steep tariff imposed on foreign tobacco. With imported leaf effectively unusable, the Mexican cigar had no choice but to be an all-Mexican-leaf puro. When the restriction was eased in 1996, the country's makers at last gained freedom to blend. The Turrents thereafter stepped into designs combining foreign leaf in A. Turrent and Casa Turrent, the official site noting that \"A. Turrent cigars are made from our own leaf together with imported tobaccos\". On the other hand, as the 2024 edition of Te-Amo returned to market trading on being a \"Mexican puro\", the old shackle is now being resold as character.【Today — Two Faces: Leaf Supplier and Maker of Finished Cigars】Alejandro Turrent says of San Andrés leaf that \"it grows nowhere but here\", grounding the firm's advantage in the valley's particular microclimate and soil. The Negro San Andrés strain, of indigenous descent, is indeed in strong demand from makers in Nicaragua and the Dominican Republic and is exported as binder and wrapper. On annual output, Alejandro is on record as saying \"last year we made roughly five million cigars\". In 2014 Alberto Turrent received Cigar Journal's Lifetime Achievement Award. Backstage supplier to the world's cigars in leaf, and its own country's largest maker of finished cigars — this duality is the outline of the company that is Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos.【Reservations on Uncertain Points】(1) No primary source confirming the year of incorporation or registration was found; 1880 is the year the family began cultivation, not the company's founding year. (2) Sources divide on whether Te-Amo's starting point is 1963 (company website) or 1966 (English-language sources), and consistency with the account of 1964 US market entry is unverified. (3) Whether \"Nueva Matacapan de Tabacos\" and \"Tabacalera Alberto Turrent\" are one legal entity under two names, or related but distinct companies, could not be settled from registry information. (4) The planted areas (Habano Criollo 40 ha, etc.) are figures from the time of reporting; the present scale is unknown. (5) The figure of five million cigars a year likewise dates from the statement, its reference year unidentifiable. (6) Employee numbers, the share of women and the count of communities rest on Mexican press articles, without corroboration from official statistics. (7) As to which foreign brands the company specifically supplies with leaf, nothing beyond the general statement \"a component of many popular brands\" could be confirmed at the level of named companies.",
      vitolas: [
        "Te-Amo series (Te-Amo; brand owned by Altadis U.S.A., made by the company. The 2024 relaunch, Te-Amo The Original San Andres Valley Cigar, comprises three formats: Robusto 5 × 54, Toro 6 × 52 and Magnum 6 × 60)",
        "Casa Turrent (the premium line the Turrent family has distributed itself since 2015: the 1880 series, Serie 1901, Serie 1942, Serie 1973, Origins and others)",
        "A. Turrent (the core line combining estate and imported leaf; Reserva de la Casa and others)",
        "Matacán (the all-Mexican-leaf budget bundle brand; covered in its own entry in this encyclopaedia)",
        "Andrea's (a brand the official site lists as its own; little further information)",
        "Legado San Andrés (a collection sold through the official online shop; a 12-cigar Robusto 50 × 5 sampler, among others)",
        "Leaf (raw material): San Andrés Negro (wrapper/binder for maduros), Habano Criollo, Sumatra-seed and Connecticut-seed"
      ],
      trivia: "【A Company Named After Ruins】Nueva Matacapan means \"New Matacapan\"; Matacapan is a Classic-period site held to be Teotihuacan's trading outpost on the Gulf, so the company name carries the memory of a trading post two thousand years old. 【A State Corporation in the Address】The factory's street name, Avenida Tabamex, derives from Tabacos Mexicanos, S.A. (Tabamex), the state tobacco corporation founded in 1972; it was privatised in Veracruz in the early 1990s, its assets passing to producer organisations. 【The Leaf May Be More Famous than the Cigars】Though the largest grower of the San Andrés leaf that becomes the wrapper of maduro cigars worldwide, the company's finished products are known chiefly at home and in parts of North America; its presence backstage is the greater. 【Six Generations】Counting from Alberto I in 1880, the current head Alberto is reckoned the fifth generation and Alejandro the sixth. 【Lifetime Achievement】In 2014 Alberto Turrent received Cigar Journal's Lifetime Achievement Award; when he entered the family business at eighteen, he already bore a family tradition of more than a century."
    },
    "Aromas de San Andrés": {
      founded: "No primary source gives a launch year. The factory of the maker, Puros Santa Clara, S.A. de C.V., opened in 1968, and the company positions Aromas de San Andrés as one of its flagship brands (the classic line)",
      founder: "Puros Santa Clara, the Ortiz family firm led by Jorge Ortiz Alvarez, of San Andrés Tuxtla, Veracruz. The individual who conceived the brand is unknown",
      meaning: "Spanish for \"aromas of San Andrés\" — the brand name is itself a declaration of origin. The San Andrés valley of southern Veracruz is Mexico's foremost cigar-tobacco region, and the name enshrines outright the company's banner of \"100 per cent Mexican tobacco\"",
      strength: "Mild–Medium. Against the 'classical, toast-laden character' of its sister marque Santa Clara 1830, the Aromas is pitched towards the smooth and creamy. Coffee, nuts, wood, earth and gentle spice are cited, and it is described as 'soft, but with character'",
      status: "Active. As one corner of Puros Santa Clara's classic line it circulates widely through Mexican specialist and online shops, with retail sightings in Europe (Denmark, Romania and elsewhere) and India. All-Mexican leaf, with a strong character as a tubed gift product",
      history: "【Santa Clara's Other Flagship】Aromas de San Andrés is a cigar marque made by Puros Santa Clara of San Andrés Tuxtla, Veracruz. The company is best known for Santa Clara 1830, which claims one of Mexico's oldest lineages; the other half of the \"classic line\" standing beside the 1830 is this Aromas de San Andrés. The official site presents the marque as \"the fruit of the finest tobaccos, raised from selected seed in the San Andrés valley of Veracruz, rolled by hand by none but the best artisans for the most discerning smokers\". The name means, literally, \"the aromas of San Andrés\" — the name of the origin adopted directly as trademark, an exceedingly plain and honest christening. Considering how often Mexican cigars are spoken of \"only as the source of San Andrés leaf\", there is a certain mettle in raising the region's name squarely and still selling a finished cigar.【The Contrast with 1830 — Toast for Santa Clara, Cream for Aromas】The company's two flagships are sharply divided in character. Where Santa Clara 1830 holds a classic, robust line of flavour built on toasted notes, Aromas de San Andrés is placed on the smooth, creamy, gentler and more approachable side. A Mexican enthusiasts' review of the robusto format notes flavours of coffee, nuts, wood, earth and gentle spice, calling it \"soft, but with character\". In strength it stands at mild to medium. The dark leaf of San Andrés readily calls to mind heavyweight maduros, but this marque is rather an attempt to draw a gentle expression from the same valley's leaf.【The Blend — All San Andrés Leaf】The specification is all-Mexican. According to retail material and enthusiast reviews, the wrapper is a light San Andrés leaf (capa clara), the binder a dark leaf called San Andrés morrón obscuro, and the filler a long-filler combination of standard and criollo San Andrés tobaccos. The Gourmet line is also described with a construction including criollo maduro, and natural and maduro strains both exist. Pairing light and dark between wrapper and binder is a device Santa Clara also uses in the 1830, and in this fine grading — leaf from the same volcanic-soiled valley sorted by colour and strength — the factory's craft is displayed.【Gourmet and Imperial — the Tube as Product Design】The most distinctive feature of Aromas de San Andrés lies in its dress as merchandise. Of the two principal forms, the Aromas Gourmet, a lonsdale-leaning 6 1/2 × 42, is housed one by one in amber glass tubes, wrapped in gold-foil pouches and packed in Spanish-cedar boxes. The Aromas Imperial, a 7 × 50 churchill, is sealed in gilded aluminium tubes designed \"so that the aromas of San Andrés do not escape\". A robusto format (around 5 × 50) is also offered in aluminium tubes, with boxes of 24 the basic unit. The tubed format favours keeping condition in markets where humidity control is difficult, while at the same time producing a handsome gift; behind the marque's long-standing choice as a souvenir and present within Mexico lies precisely this design thinking.【Assessment — Rustic Looks, Substance for the Price】Third-party assessments are few. In one of the rare specialist reviews that can be confirmed, the Mexican enthusiast site Humo y Tabaco examined the robusto in 2018, writing frankly of its appearance — \"countless bumps, veins and uneven firmness; it looks extremely rustic and cheap\" — while concluding this fell short of a defect. The review's score was 71, by no means high; but considered against the price bracket in which the marque sits, the verdict is less unjust than realistic. Aromas de San Andrés is not a marque contending in the premium tier: it is designed as a product filling the space between the everyday and the gift counter of its home region.【Distribution — from Mexico to Europe and Asia】Puros Santa Clara produces on the order of 160,000 cigars a month and exports to at least fifteen countries, the Mexican press reports. Aromas de San Andrés rides that current, its name remaining on the lists of a Danish pipe specialist, a Romanian cigar retailer and an Indian cigar club. At home it is stocked by specialist shops such as cigar boutiques and the Noble Tabaco Club, and can be had by the box or the single stick. Its fame in the world's premium cigar market is limited, but in the market that seeks \"a finished Mexican cigar\" it circulates dependably.【The Stage: the San Andrés Valley】Indispensable to understanding this marque is the standing of its home region. San Andrés Tuxtla is the home of Mexico's finest cigar tobaccos and the centre of the country's cigar exports; its dense volcanic soils give rise to dark leaf of singular flavour. In recent years the valley's leaf has won worldwide popularity as the \"San Andrés wrapper\" rolled onto celebrated Nicaraguan and Dominican brands — but that popularity is strictly as \"raw material used in other countries' cigars\". Aromas de San Andrés takes that material, works it into cigars with its own hands and sells it under the region's name, embodying Santa Clara's refusal to remain a mere supplier of raw material.【The Memory of an All-Mexican Regime】That Aromas de San Andrés flies the flag of \"100 per cent Mexican tobacco\" carries a historical charge beyond mere boasting of origin. In twentieth-century Mexico, high tariffs on foreign tobacco made cigar making with imported leaf practically impossible; as a result, Mexican cigars were compelled to be \"puros\", Mexican in everything from wrapper to filler. The restraint was eased in 1996, after which the country's makers gained the freedom to combine foreign leaf — it was in just this aftermath that Cruz Real turned to Connecticut-shade wrappers and the new marque Excelsior appeared dressed in foreign leaf. Amid all this, Santa Clara kept its flagship classic line entirely Mexican, accepting an imposed constraint as its own style. Recalling how from 2021 Casa 1910 seized the international spotlight under the banner of the \"premium Mexican puro\", the choice proved, in the event, far-sighted; Aromas de San Andrés is the gentler face born of that conservatism.【The Factory Behind It — Hand Rolling and Visitors】The maker, Puros Santa Clara, keeps its workshop in the town of San Andrés Tuxtla and receives visitors, serving also as a tourist attraction. The Mexican press describes it as a family firm with \"some 194 years of cigar-making history\", giving figures of around 160,000 cigars a month and exports to more than fifteen countries. By the standards of the world's great factories, 160,000 a month is by no means large, but for a workshop finishing everything by hand it is a considerable scale. Aromas de San Andrés occupies its corner of that production, emerging from the hands of the same artisans as its stablemates Santa Clara 1830, the Santa Clara Special Edition and Veracruz Premium. The brands are not divided among separate factories: at the same benches, leaf from the same valley is recombined by colour and strength into different faces — the mildness of Aromas de San Andrés is the product of that artisanal sorting.【Reservations on Uncertain Points】(1) No primary source was found for the brand's launch year, its deviser or the circumstances of its naming; there is no basis for equating it with the factory's opening year (1968). (2) The blend specification (the morrón obscuro binder, etc.) rests on retail material and enthusiast reviews; no official disclosure of the blend by the maker has been confirmed. (3) The full range of formats is unknown; those confirmed are roughly the Gourmet (6 1/2 × 42), Imperial (7 × 50) and Robusto (around 5 × 50), and even the robusto's dimensions waver between 5 × 50 and 5 × 54 by source. (4) The figures of 160,000 a month and fifteen export countries date from the time of reporting; current values are unknown. (5) How the natural and maduro strains correspond to the formats has not been resolved. (6) Third-party assessment is exceedingly scarce, and the description of taste depends on retail copy and a single review. (7) Products of identical or similar names (including variant spellings such as \"Aromas de San Andreas\") exist with several retailers, and it has not been confirmed that all are the same brand.",
      vitolas: [
        "Aromas Gourmet (6 1/2 × 42; each cigar housed in an amber glass tube, wrapped in gold foil and packed in a Spanish-cedar box of 24)",
        "Aromas Imperial (7 × 50 churchill format; gilded aluminium tube)",
        "Aromas Robusto (around 5 × 50; commonly sold singly in aluminium tubes; some sources give 5 × 54)",
        "Aromas Robusto Tubo de Cristal (a boxed presentation in glass tubes, circulating in Mexican domestic retail)",
        "Natural and Maduro strains (differing wrapper finishes; both wholly San Andrés leaf)",
        "Common blend: light San Andrés wrapper, San Andrés morrón obscuro binder, and long filler combining standard and criollo San Andrés leaf"
      ],
      trivia: "【A Name that Declares Its Origin】\"Aromas de San Andrés\" — \"the aromas of San Andrés\": the region's name adopted as trademark, a christening without hesitation. 【Glass Tubes and Gold Foil】The Gourmet dresses each cigar in an amber glass tube, wraps it in a gold-foil pouch and packs it in a Spanish-cedar box — a design aiming at once at condition-keeping in markets where humidity control is hard and at presence as a gift. 【Toasted 1830, Creamy Aromas】The same factory's two flagships are opposites in character: against the classic, robust 1830, Aromas is swung to the smooth and creamy side. 【71 Points】In its 2018 robusto review the Mexican enthusiast site Humo y Tabaco wrote frankly that the cigar \"looks extremely rustic and cheap\" and scored it 71 — less harsh than realistic, given the price bracket. 【No Mere Supplier】San Andrés leaf is famed worldwide as the wrapper of maduro brands, but that is fame as \"raw material for other countries' cigars\". This marque works the same leaf into cigars itself and sells them under the region's name."
    }
  };
  BRANDS_DATA.mexico.forEach(function (b) {
    var e = EN[b.en];
    if (!e) return;
    if (e.vitolas && b.vitolas && e.vitolas.length !== b.vitolas.length) delete e.vitolas;
    Object.assign(b, e);
  });
})();
